##CHANNEL GROWTH AND REVIVAL 

Publishing videos consistently is only one part of running a YouTube channel. You also need to monitor performance, understand your audience and improve your content strategy over time.

If your channel stops growing or your videos receive fewer views, you should identify the underlying problems before making major changes.





1. Monitor Your Channel Performance

Regularly review your channel's performance using YouTube Studio.

How to do it:

1. Open YouTube Studio.
2. Navigate to the Analytics section.
3. Review views, impressions and click-through rate (CTR).
4. Monitor average view duration and audience retention.
5. Identify which videos perform well and which struggle to attract viewers.
6. Look for patterns in topics, titles, thumbnails and audience behaviour.

Use these insights to understand what your audience prefers and improve future uploads.





2. Diagnose a Stalled Channel

A stalled channel is one that experiences little growth or a decline in views and engagement.

Before making changes, review your existing content and historical performance.

How to do it:

1. Analyse your older videos and identify which topics previously performed well.
2. Review audience retention and CTR to identify possible weaknesses.
3. Check whether your recent content is still relevant to your target audience.
4. Identify whether your channel needs improvements in presentation, topics or niche direction.
5. Develop a plan based on your findings rather than making random changes.





3. Preserve Your Historical Data

When attempting to revive a channel, avoid deleting large numbers of old videos immediately.

The field guide recommends preserving historical data and using the following approach:

- Avoid bulk-deleting existing videos.
- Review older uploads before making changes.
- Consider unlisting or setting videos to private when you no longer want them publicly visible.
- Preserve useful performance information for future analysis.

This allows you to retain your historical channel information while making changes to your content direction.






4. Follow a Four-Month Sub-Niche Pivot Strategy

If you decide to move towards a different sub-niche, the field guide suggests gradually changing your content over four months.

Month| Old/Core Niche| New Sub-Niche
Month 1| 75%| 25%
Month 2| 50%| 50%
Month 3| 25%| 75%
Month 4| 0%| 100%

How to do it:

1. Begin by publishing mostly content from your existing niche.
2. Gradually introduce videos from the new sub-niche.
3. Increase the proportion of new content each month.
4. Monitor how viewers respond to the transition.
5. Complete the shift towards the new sub-niche by the fourth month.

This is a suggested transition framework from the guide, not a guaranteed method for reviving a channel.







5. Maintain a Production Buffer

Consistency becomes difficult when you create every video immediately before its publishing date.

A production buffer helps you maintain a regular upload schedule.

How to do it:

1. Decide on a realistic publishing frequency.
2. Prepare upcoming video topics and scripts in advance.
3. Complete video production before the scheduled publishing date.
4. Keep a few finished videos ready for unexpected delays.
5. Continue replenishing your buffer as you publish new videos.

For example, if you publish one video every seven days, maintain a backup of three completed evergreen videos.

This gives you approximately three weeks of backup content.






Things to Keep in Mind

- Analyse performance before changing your content strategy.
- Avoid deleting videos impulsively.
- Preserve useful historical performance data.
- Introduce a new sub-niche gradually when appropriate.
- Maintain a realistic and consistent publishing schedule.
- Keep backup videos ready to avoid interruptions.
- Remember that channel growth takes time and is not guaranteed.

Useful Tools

- YouTube Studio: Monitor channel performance and audience analytics.
- Google Sheets: Track video performance and publishing schedules.
- Notion: Organise video ideas, production tasks and content calendars.

Expected Outcome

By completing this step, you should have a system for monitoring channel performance, identifying growth problems, planning a sub-niche transition and maintaining a consistent publishing schedule.
